        </nav>
        <div class="article-intro">
          <h1>Ozzit @@NAME@@ functions</h1>
          <p class="lead-note">The @@COUNT@@ @@NAME@@ formulas cover @@COVERS@@. Each entry gives what the workbook’s own help supplies: the signature, parameters, notes and worked examples.</p>
          <p class="release-meta">Release @@TAG@@. Read from the released <a href="@@SOURCE@@">@@NAME@@ formula source</a>, which the workbook compiles.</p>
          <p class="page-meta">Published @@PUBLISHED@@. Last reviewed @@PUBLISHED@@.</p>
        </div>
      </header>
      <div class="article-layout site-shell">
        <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="On this page">
          <p>On this page</p>
          <a href="#use">Using these functions</a>
          <a href="#functions">All @@COUNT@@ functions</a>
          <a href="#modules">Other modules</a>
        </nav>
        <div class="article-body">

          <section id="use">
            <h2>Using these functions</h2>
            <p>Each named formula is a native Excel <code>LAMBDA</code> in the free Ozzit workbook, or one of its 5 help tables. It needs Microsoft 365 or Excel 2024 or later. Enter a call in a cell of the workbook, or copy the definition from its name manager into your own model. Leave room for any spilled result.</p>
            <p>The text below is the help each function carries in the @@TAG@@ workbook. The workbook remains the authority, and a calculation does not decide an accounting treatment. <a href="/tools/ozzit/#download">Download the workbook and check its hash</a>.</p>
          </section>

          <section id="functions">
            <h2>All @@COUNT@@ functions</h2>
@@ENTRIES@@          </section>

          <section id="modules">
            <h2>Other modules</h2>
            <ul class="related">
@@OTHERS@@              <li><a href="/tools/ozzit/">Ozzit overview, download and release verification</a></li>
            </ul>
          </section>

          <p class="byline">
            Written by Ryan Duguid in his own time and on his own equipment.
            He is a Provisional CA ANZ Member.
          </p>
        </div>
